<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=euclids-elements">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euclid's Elements</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>'s Elements is a mathematical treatise in 13 books written about 300 BCE by the Greek mathematician Euclid, the oldest surviving large-scale deductive treatment of mathematics. From definitions, postulates and common notions it proves theorems of plane and solid geometry and number theory, among them the Pythagorean theorem and the infinity of the primes. Translated into Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and first printed in 1482, it has been called the most successful textbook ever written; Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euclids-elements.jpg" alt="Euclid's Elements" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: University of Pennsylvania Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P.Penn._Museum_inv._E02748_Euclid's_Elements_2.5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 300 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Alexandria</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a> · <a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>It is largely a compilation of earlier Greek mathematics, and how much of it Euclid wrote himself is debated.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/heliocentrism">Heliocentrism<span>c. 270 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">Astrolabe<span>4th century CE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">Algebra<span>825</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-optics">Book of Optics<span>1011–1021</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/calculus">Calculus<span>1665–1666</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=euclids-elements">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
